# Fingerprints on relations

A reference is what an author writes to point at a section: an anchor in code, which composes the section, or an anchor in prose, which links to it. The author who wrote the reference read the section as it stood on the day. When the section changes later, nothing tells the author that the reference may no longer hold.

A relation is the edge Loom derives from a reference, from the place where the reference stands to the section it points to. This chapter gives each relation a fingerprint. Loom keeps what a section looked like when the author last approved a reference to it, and it reports the reference as drifted once the section no longer matches. So no code and no prose drifts in silence from a section it depends on.

## A relation

A `Relation` is found by Loom's own names: the `loom` and the `section` that hold the reference, the `kind` of reference, and the name the reference writes, which the relation carries as `to`. Beside its names it says where the reference stands, as `at`, and which section the reference points to, as `target`.

A relation has a status, which Loom works out each time and never stores:

- **`untracked`:** nobody has approved the reference, so Loom keeps no fingerprint for it.
- **`inStep`:** the section still matches the fingerprint Loom keeps.
- **`drifted`:** the section no longer matches it.

A `KeptRelation` is what the workspace keeps of a relation: its names, and the `fingerprint` of its section as the author approved it. Nothing else of a relation is kept.

=>

export const KeptRelationSchema = Schema.Struct({
  loom: Schema.String,
  section: Schema.String,
  kind: Schema.String,
  to: Schema.String,
  fingerprint: Schema.String,
})
export type KeptRelation = typeof KeptRelationSchema.Type

export type RelationStatus = 'untracked' | 'inStep' | 'drifted'

export type Relation = {
  readonly loom: Location
  readonly section: string
  readonly kind: string
  readonly to: string
  readonly at: Position
  readonly target: SectionRef
  readonly status: RelationStatus
}

## The references that make relations

Two kinds of reference make a relation. An `anchor` is an anchor in code, which composes a section or names its module. A `link` is an anchor in prose, which links to a section. A box that points to a chapter makes none, since the box already copies a summary of what it points to and goes stale with it, as the chapter "Boxes" describes.

A reference makes a relation only when it points into another loom. Inside one loom the prose and the code of every section sit together under the author's eyes, and a section that assembles a file refers to every section it composes. A fingerprint there would mark those references at every edit.

The kinds are a table, `relationKinds`. Each entry holds a kind's name and how to find its references in a loom: for each, the section that holds it, the name it writes, where it stands, and the section it points to. A further kind of reference joins by adding an entry, and the relations it makes are told from the others by their `kind`.

=>

type Found = Pick<Relation, 'section' | 'to' | 'at' | 'target'>

export type RelationKind = {
  readonly name: string
  readonly referencesIn: (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule) => ReadonlyArray<Found>
}

const inAnotherLoom =
  (module: LoomModule) =>
  (target: SectionRef): boolean =>
    target.module !== module.path

~

An anchor in code reaches the product as an edge. An edge the loom bound to one of its own sections stays inside the loom, so `anchorsIn` passes it by. Any other edge resolves through the book, as the chapter "The product pass" describes, and `openEdge` keeps it when it resolves to one section of another loom.

=>

type Edge = {
  readonly name: string
  readonly target: Option.Option<unknown>
  readonly anchor: Position
}

const openEdge =
  (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule, section: string) =>
  (edge: Edge): ReadonlyArray<Found> =>
    Option.isSome(edge.target)
      ? []
      : pipe(
          resolveName(catalogue, module.path, edge.name),
          Option.filter(inAnotherLoom(module)),
          Option.map((target): Found => ({ section, to: edge.name, at: edge.anchor, target })),
          Option.toArray,
        )

const anchorsIn = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Found> =>
  Array.flatMap(module.product.code, (host) =>
    Array.flatMap(host.fragments, (part) =>
      Match.value(part).pipe(
        Match.when({ type: 'NameRef' }, openEdge(catalogue, module, host.origin.name)),
        Match.when({ type: 'ModuleRef' }, openEdge(catalogue, module, host.origin.name)),
        Match.when({ type: 'Fragment' }, (): ReadonlyArray<Found> => []),
        Match.exhaustive,
      ),
    ),
  )

~

A link stands in the prose of a section. `linksIn` resolves each link of each section and keeps the ones that point into another loom.

=>

const linksIn = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Found> =>
  Array.flatMap(module.doc.sections, (section) =>
    Array.flatMap(sectionLinksOf(section), (link) =>
      pipe(
        resolveAnchor(catalogue, module.path, link),
        Option.filter(inAnotherLoom(module)),
        Option.map(
          (target): Found => ({
            section: sectionNameOf(section),
            to: link.name.value,
            at: link.name.position,
            target,
          }),
        ),
        Option.toArray,
      ),
    ),
  )

export const relationKinds: ReadonlyArray<RelationKind> = [
  { name: 'anchor', referencesIn: anchorsIn },
  { name: 'link', referencesIn: linksIn },
]

## A fingerprint says whether, never where

The fingerprint of a section is a hash of the section's text, its prose and its code together. Blank lines and spaces at the end of a line do not count, so a section that was only tidied keeps its fingerprint. `fingerprintOf` hashes the text that `settled` gives.

A fingerprint answers one question: is this still the text the author approved. It never finds anything. Tokens say where a reference stands, and a relation's names say which relation it is.

=>

const settled = (text: string): string =>
  pipe(
    text.split('\n'),
    Array.map((line) => line.trimEnd()),
    Array.filter((line) => line !== ''),
    Array.join('\n'),
  )

export const fingerprintOf = (section: LoomSection): string =>
  createHash('sha256').update(settled(section.source)).digest('hex')

## The relations of a loom

`relationsOf` derives the relations of a loom: one for each reference the table finds, with the status that the kept relations give it. A relation is matched to what is kept by its names alone, which `keyOf` joins. `statusOf` compares the fingerprint kept under those names, when there is one, with the fingerprint the section has now.

=>

type Named = Pick<Relation, 'loom' | 'section' | 'kind' | 'to'>

const keyOf = (named: Named): string =>
  JSON.stringify([named.loom, named.section, named.kind, named.to])

const statusOf = (kept: Option.Option<string>, section: LoomSection): RelationStatus =>
  Option.match(kept, {
    onNone: () => 'untracked',
    onSome: (fingerprint) => (fingerprint === fingerprintOf(section) ? 'inStep' : 'drifted'),
  })

export const relationsOf = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
): ReadonlyArray<Relation> => {
  const fingerprints = new Map(
    Array.map(kept, (held) => [keyOf(held), held.fingerprint] as const),
  )
  return Array.flatMap(relationKinds, (kind) =>
    Array.map(kind.referencesIn(catalogue, module), (found): Relation => {
      const named: Named = {
        loom: module.path,
        section: found.section,
        kind: kind.name,
        to: found.to,
      }
      return {
        ...named,
        at: found.at,
        target: found.target,
        status: statusOf(
          Option.fromNullishOr(fingerprints.get(keyOf(named))),
          found.target.section,
        ),
      }
    }),
  )
}

## Drift

`driftFaults` gives each drifted relation of a loom the `DriftedReference` fault, on the reference itself. The fault is a warning. A tangle goes ahead all the same, since a drifted reference still composes.

An untracked relation carries nothing. So a book that starts to use fingerprints does not light up, and an author takes them up one reference at a time.

Loom keeps a fingerprint and no text of its own. So it says that a section changed, and never what changed in it. The author reads the section as it stands now, and a book kept under git shows there what the section said before.

=>

export const driftFaults = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  pipe(
    relationsOf(catalogue, module, kept),
    Array.filter((relation) => relation.status === 'drifted'),
    Array.flatMap(
      (relation) => faulty(DriftedReference({ name: relation.to }), relation.at).diagnostics,
    ),
  )

## What changed, and what it left behind

A fault stands on each drifted reference, which suits an author who is reading the loom that holds it. An author who reviews asks the opposite question: which sections changed, and what did each leave behind. One changed section may have drifted many references, in many looms.

A `Drift` answers for one changed section. It names the section by its loom and by its title as the author wrote it, and it holds the relations that drifted from the section. `driftsIn` groups the drifted relations it is handed by the section they point to, and passes the others by. The relations may come from any number of looms, so a review of a folder groups across all of them. The groups come in the order their first relation was handed in.

=>

export type Drift = {
  readonly loom: Location
  readonly section: string
  readonly relations: ReadonlyArray<Relation>
}

const changedSection = (relation: Relation): string =>
  JSON.stringify([relation.target.module, sectionNameOf(relation.target.section)])

const titleOf = (section: LoomSection): string =>
  section.heading.title?.source ?? sectionNameOf(section)

export const driftsIn = (relations: ReadonlyArray<Relation>): ReadonlyArray<Drift> =>
  pipe(
    Array.filter(relations, (relation) => relation.status === 'drifted'),
    Array.groupBy(changedSection),
    Record.values,
    Array.map((drifted): Drift => {
      const { target } = Array.headNonEmpty(drifted)
      return { loom: target.module, section: titleOf(target.section), relations: drifted }
    }),
  )

## Approving

Approving a reference keeps the fingerprint of its section as the section stands now. The author has read the section and the reference still holds.

An approval covers the references its author chose and no others. The choice is a test on a relation. `chosenRelations` gives what to keep for each chosen relation of a loom, with the fingerprint its section has now, which `keptOf` reads. Two references in one section that write the same name make one relation, so choosing either of them approves it.

`approvedRelations` gives everything to keep after an approval. That is what was chosen, and beside it every kept relation that still stands: one of another loom, and one of this loom whose reference still exists and was not chosen, drifted or not. A kept relation of the loom whose reference is gone does not stand, so what is kept holds the references that exist and no history. An untracked relation that was not chosen stays untracked.

What is kept comes sorted by its names, so two authors who approve different looms change different lines of the file that keeps it.

=>

const byNames: Order.Order<KeptRelation> = Order.mapInput(Order.String, keyOf)

const sameNames = (one: Named, other: Named): boolean => keyOf(one) === keyOf(other)

const keptOf = (relation: Relation): KeptRelation => ({
  loom: relation.loom,
  section: relation.section,
  kind: relation.kind,
  to: relation.to,
  fingerprint: fingerprintOf(relation.target.section),
})

export const chosenRelations = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
  chosen: (relation: Relation) => boolean,
): ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation> =>
  pipe(
    Array.filter(relationsOf(catalogue, module, kept), chosen),
    Array.map(keptOf),
    Array.dedupeWith(sameNames),
  )

export const approvedRelations = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
  chosen: (relation: Relation) => boolean,
): ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation> => {
  const fresh = chosenRelations(catalogue, module, kept, chosen)
  const present = new Set(Array.map(relationsOf(catalogue, module, kept), keyOf))
  const stands = (held: KeptRelation): boolean =>
    held.loom !== module.path ||
    (present.has(keyOf(held)) && !Array.some(fresh, (approved) => sameNames(approved, held)))
  return pipe([...Array.filter(kept, stands), ...fresh], Array.sort(byNames))
}

## What relations draw on

The corpus model gives the catalogue, a loom's module, the links of a section, and the two functions that resolve a name and an anchor to a section. The product pass gives a section's name, and the fault catalog gives `faulty` and the `DriftedReference` fault. Node's `createHash` computes the hash. Effect supplies `Schema` for the relation, and `Array`, `Match`, `Option`, `Order`, `Record`, and `pipe` for the rest.

=>

import { createHash } from 'node:crypto'
import { Array, Match, Option, Order, pipe, Record, Schema } from 'effect'
import type { Diagnostic, Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { LoomSection } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import {
  type Catalogue,
  type Location,
  type LoomModule,
  resolveAnchor,
  resolveName,
  sectionLinksOf,
  type SectionRef,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { DriftedReference, faulty } from '#ast/LoomFault'
import { sectionNameOf } from '#ast/ProductBuilder'

