::[Drift among a loom's problems]

## Where relations are kept

Relations are kept in the workspace, in the file `.loom/relations.lock`, apart from `loom.lock`. A tangle rewrites `loom.lock` on every run, while a relation changes only when an author approves, so keeping them apart keeps both files' differences small.

The file is JSON, one line for each relation, in the order `approvedRelations` gives. Loom reads and writes it through the `WorkspaceSource` service, as the chapter "Where a workspace is kept" describes. Remote storage hands relations over with a workspace and takes no write.

`loom approve` names what to approve by a path, from one reference up to the corpus, and writes the file, as the chapter "The command-line program" describes. `billing.loom:12` is the reference on a line, `billing.loom` is every reference of a loom, and a folder is every reference of the looms it holds.

`loom review` takes the same path and lists what has drifted under it, one group for each changed section. With `--json` it prints the same groups for an agent, and each reference in them carries the path and line that `loom approve` takes. With `--check` it fails when anything has drifted, so a CI step can hold a book to its approvals.

## A reference renamed by hand

A relation is found by its names, so a reference that changes its name leaves its relation behind. The renamed reference has no relation, and Loom says nothing about it, as it says nothing about any reference nobody approved. The author chose to change the reference. The next approval of the loom drops the relation left behind and records the reference under its new name.

## What this chapter grows into

1. In the editor: a quick fix that approves one reference, and one that starts tracking a reference.
2. The editor's rename of a section, which rewrites the references to it, carrying their relations along.
